.lang wikidata

# Disjointness violations, read from Wikidata's own definitions.
#
# Wikidata states class disjointness as a `disjoint union of` (P2738) statement
# whose members sit in `list item` (P11260) qualifiers. Those live in the
# STATEMENT layer, which `.wikidata-qualifiers` adds on top of an already
# loaded network, so the predicates are the prefixed node names `p:P2738` and
# `pq:P11260` -- not the bare IDs. A rule written with the bare names matches
# nothing at all and reports a clean graph:
#
#   .load wikidata-20260309-all-pruned-small.bin
#   .wikidata-qualifiers wikidata-20260309-all.json.bz2 P11260
#   .import dev_scripts/wikidata-disjointness-violations
#   .run
#
# The A != B guard is what makes the rule say what it means. Without it both
# variables bind to the SAME list member, the two class conditions collapse
# into one, and every class below any listed member is reported as a violation
# -- a report that looks thorough and is noise. The SPARQL form of this query
# has always carried the guard, as FILTER(str(?disj1) < str(?disj2)).
#
# A and B range over the members of one list, in both orders, so each violation
# arrives twice. The premise SET is the same both times, and a contradiction is
# recorded per set, so it is reported once.

(*{(C "p:P2738" S) (S "pq:P11260" A) (S "pq:P11260" B) (A != B) (K P279 A) (K P279 B)} ~ conjunction) => !
(*{(C "p:P2738" S) (S "pq:P11260" A) (S "pq:P11260" B) (A != B) (X P31 A)  (X P31 B)}  ~ conjunction) => !
